  "headline": "Oil-led inflation scare pushes yields and the dollar to highs while growth holds",
  "body": [
    "The configuration reads as an inflation shock, not a growth scare. Brent at $102 is up 6% this week and 37% over three months, and the bond market is pricing it as a reason for policy to stay tight: the 10-year yield has risen 49 basis points over the month to 5.27%, within a whisker of its 52-week high, while the 30-year has added only 40, flattening the 30y–10y spread by 9 basis points. Rising yields with a flattening curve is the signature of a market bracing for tighter money, and the dollar index sitting at the top of its yearly range, up 2.9% on the month, confirms it.",
    "The growth leg is intact, which is what separates this from stagflation. Copper holds near the top of its range, up 18% year-to-date, and the copper/gold ratio has climbed 8% over the month. Gold's 6.7% monthly fall alongside higher yields says real rates, not fear, are in charge, and bitcoin outrunning gold by 12.5% over the month shows risk appetite has not been withdrawn.",
    "The tension is in the currencies. USD/CAD at a 52-week high with oil above $100 means the dollar's strength and the inflation reading of the oil move outweigh the usual petro-currency bid; USD/INR near its high says importers are feeling it. Today's 4-basis-point dip in yields and softer dollar is a pause within that trend, not a change of it."
  ],
